 ## Overview
-Heightfield Rendering is a CPU-driven, image-order technique for rendering 2D heightmap terrains by casting rays from the camera through discrete depth planes (view-Z slices) and projecting the intersection heights onto a 2D screen buffer [S1, S2, S3, S4, unofficial]. It solves the problem of real-time terrain visualization with hidden surface removal, distinguishing itself from standard raycasting by casting multiple rays per screen column at increasing depth intervals (Level of Detail) rather than one ray per pixel, and from voxel rendering by operating on a 2D height function rather than a 3D occupancy grid [S2, S3, S4, unofficial]. Unlike [Terrain Raymarching](1357-terrain-raymarching.md), which typically marches a single ray per pixel to find the first hit, this method fills vertical screen segments from the calculated terrain height down to the horizon (or previous occlusion buffer) for each column [S2, S3, unofficial].
+Heightfield Rendering is a CPU-driven, image-order technique for rendering 2D heightmap terrains by casting rays from the camera through discrete depth planes (view-Z slices) and projecting the intersection heights onto a 2D screen buffer [S4, unofficial]. It solves the problem of real-time terrain visualization with hidden surface removal, distinguishing itself from standard raycasting by casting multiple rays per screen column at increasing depth intervals (Level of Detail) rather than one ray per pixel, and from voxel rendering by operating on a 2D height function rather than a 3D occupancy grid [S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. Unlike [Terrain Raymarching](1357-terrain-raymarching.md), which typically marches a single ray per pixel to find the first hit, this method fills vertical screen segments from the calculated terrain height down to the horizon (or previous occlusion buffer) for each column [S2, unofficial].
 
